<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Anthem · 1528 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn that Martin Luther wrote and set to music around 1528. It became the best-known song of the Protestant Reformation.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Martin Luther</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Martin Luther</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Lutheran and wider Protestant churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Wittenberg, Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>between 1527 and 1529</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Martin Luther (d. 1546))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, the Protestant Reformer and a prolific writer of hymns, wrote both the words and the tune of &quot;Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott&quot; between 1527 and 1529. The words are mostly his own, although the opening paraphrases Psalm 46. The earliest hymnal that survives with the hymn is Andrew Rauscher's of 1531. It is believed to have appeared in Joseph Klug's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> hymnal of 1529 under the title of the 46th Psalm, and before that in Hans Weiss's Wittenberg hymnal of 1528, but no copy of either remains. Because Luther's hymns were printed soon after he wrote them, these lost books support the dating to 1527–1529.</p><p>Why Luther wrote it is not known. The hymnologist John Julian recorded four theories: that Luther and his companions sang it entering Worms for the Diet in April 1521 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine">Heinrich Heine</a>); that it honoured Luther's friend Leonhard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>, executed on 16 August 1527 (K. F. T. Schneider); that the Lutheran princes sang it entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> for the Diet of 1530 (Jean-Henri Merle d'Aubigné); or that it belongs to the Diet of Speyer of 1529, where the Lutheran princes protested against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s wish to enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-worms">Edict of Worms</a>. The historian John M. Merriman suggests instead that it began as a martial song against the Ottoman forces. Nineteenth-century musicologists doubted that Luther wrote the tune, but the scholarly consensus now holds that he did. His original melody was strongly rhythmic; a later, even-metred version is the one most churches sing.</p><p>The hymn came to be called the &quot;Battle Hymn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>&quot;. In 1834 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine">Heinrich Heine</a> called it the Marseillaise of the Reformation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> it was also sung as a patriotic song, for example at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg-festival">Wartburg Festival</a> of 1817, since many Protestants saw the Reformation as a national as well as a religious movement. It is reported to have been a battle hymn of the Lutheran king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, an idea taken up by 19th-century poets, but no primary source supports it. It had been translated into Swedish by 1536, presumably by Olaus Petri, and in the late 19th century it became an anthem of the early Swedish socialist movement.</p><p>The hymn has been translated into English at least seventy times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myles-coverdale">Myles Coverdale</a> made the first translation in 1539; Frederick H. Hedge's of 1853 and Thomas Carlyle's became the most popular outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, while North American Lutherans long sang a composite translation from the 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> Lutheran Church Book and, more recently, that of the 1978 Lutheran Book of Worship. The hymn remains a staple of Protestant hymnbooks; it is also a suggested hymn for Catholic Masses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and appears in the Catholic Book of Worship of the Canadian Catholic Conference (1972). Since 2022 its history has been presented in the permanent exhibition of the Lutherhaus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>.</p><p>Composers have used the tune for five centuries: Bach in an organ setting (BWV 720), in four-part settings and in his chorale cantata BWV 80 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> Day; Buxtehude, Pachelbel, Telemann and Handel; Mendelssohn in the finale of his Reformation Symphony (1830); Meyerbeer in Les <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> (1836); Wagner in his Kaisermarsch (1871); and later Reger, Debussy, Glazunov, Vaughan Williams and Julius Eastman.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of trust in God as a refuge and stronghold, written in the language of siege and battle. It sets the believer's own weakness against the power of the devil and the world, and holds that God's word and kingdom will stand whatever is lost. Its martial imagery is why later listeners heard it as a song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>'s struggle.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ein feste Burg ist unser Gott,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>A strong castle is our God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ein gute Wehr und Waffen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a good defence and weapons.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er hilft uns frei aus aller Not,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He helps us free from every need</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die uns jetzt hat betroffen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that has now befallen us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der alt böse Feind,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The old evil foe</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mit Ernst er’s jetzt meint.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>now means it in earnest.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Groß Macht und viel List</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Great might and much cunning</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sein grausam Rüstung ist.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are his cruel armour.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Auf Erd ist nicht seinsgleichen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On earth there is none his equal.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mit unsrer Macht ist nichts getan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With our own might nothing is done;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wir sind gar bald verloren.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are very soon lost.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es streit’t für uns der rechte Mann,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There fights for us the right man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Den Gott hat selbst erkoren.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whom God himself has chosen.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Fragst du, wer der ist?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do you ask who that is?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er heißt Jesus Christ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He is called Jesus Christ,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Herr Zebaoth,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Lord Sabaoth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und ist kein ander Gott.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and there is no other God.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das Feld muß er behalten.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He must hold the field.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und wenn die Welt voll Teufel wär</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And if the world were full of devils</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und wollt uns gar verschlingen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and wanted to swallow us up entirely,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">So fürchten wir uns nicht so sehr,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are not so very afraid;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es soll uns doch gelingen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we shall still prevail.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Fürst dieser Welt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The prince of this world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wie saur er sich stellt,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>however grim he makes himself look,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Tut er uns doch nicht.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>can still do nothing to us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das macht, er ist gericht’t.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That is because he is judged.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ein Wörtlein kann ihn fällen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One little word can fell him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das Wort sie sollen lassen stahn</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Word they shall let stand</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und kein’ Dank dazu haben.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and have no thanks for it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Er ist bei uns wohl auf dem Plan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He is with us, indeed, on the field</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mit seinem Geist und Gaben.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with his Spirit and gifts.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Nehmen sie den Leib,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If they take the body,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gut, Ehr, Kind und Weib,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>goods, honour, child and wife,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Laß fahren dahin.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let them go.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sie haben’s kein Gewinn.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They gain nothing by it.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Das Reich muß uns doch bleiben.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The kingdom must still remain ours.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Martin Luther (1529). Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Ein_feste_Burg_ist_unser_Gott" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'Burg' is a castle or stronghold.</li><li>line 2.7: 'Zebaoth' (Sabaoth) is the Hebrew title 'of hosts', i.e. the Lord of the heavenly armies; kept as a name.</li><li>line 3.8: 'er ist gericht't' = he has been judged (condemned), after John 16:11.</li><li>line 4.2: 'kein' Dank dazu haben' is read as 'and get no thanks for it' (they must leave the Word alone, like it or not); the phrase is often glossed 'whether they like it or not'.</li><li>line 4.3: 'auf dem Plan' = on the battlefield.</li><li>line 4.7: 'Laß fahren dahin' = let it go, let it all be gone.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
